{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act — eviction causes for assault/threats, criminal-activity liability, theft and human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or a juvenile adjudicated delinquent for, assault or threats against the landlord, family or employees, theft of property, or human trafficking. A three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year limit on suit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants under the Eviction Law; the cause applies where the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside on the premises, is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is found delinquent or civilly liable for the listed offenses.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies where the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside there, is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is found delinquent or civilly liable for the listed offenses.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"Subsection (p) does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for the use or possession of drugs to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant in a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by a court order, and only for staying after the lease term, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, willful damage, constant violation of written rules, or a lease violation reserving re-entry.","key_value":"3-month, 1-month, or 3-day Notice to Quit depending on tenancy and cause","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"landlord-occupied","unit_limit":3},{"type":"units","role":"covered","op":"at_least","n":2}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions — good cause and notice","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"at least 3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless they fall within an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"units","role":"covered","op":"at_least","n":2},{"type":"other","role":"covered","text":"living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house"]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c))","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations — owner occupancy and removal from residential use","requirement":"A landlord who gave notice to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months; one who gave notice to permanently board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use must not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":"Applies to landlords who evicted tenants on the basis that the landlord would personally occupy the unit or that the building would be permanently removed from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Triggered when a tenant vacates after a notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit or permanently board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation of the five-year rule; the property may not be registered as a planned real estate development during the five-year period.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal — civil rights of tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, makes a good-faith complaint to a government authority, joins a tenant organization, or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"Applies when the tenant takes one of the listed protected actions (enforcing rights under the lease or law, a good-faith complaint to a government authority after notice to the landlord, organizing, or refusing a retaliatory lease change).","coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure — tenant rights and 90-day notice to quit","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. A buyer who wants to personally occupy must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner of a foreclosed property with rental units must notify tenants in English and Spanish within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10-business-day ownership notice","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in a property purchased at foreclosure; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to tenancies in a property purchased at foreclosure; the 90-day notice applies when the buyer wants to personally occupy the unit as a primary residence.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants 62 or older, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans who lived in a converting building at least one year before conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is at most three times county average per-person income or $50,000.","key_value":"40-year protected tenancy; income limit three times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership; tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans at least 60% disabled from service, resident at least one year before the conversion recording.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","role":"covered","text":"buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"}],"per_tenancy":"Tenant must be at least 62 years old, permanently disabled, or a veteran disabled 60% or more from service, and must have lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording; income must be at most three times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","coverage_quotes":["Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28)."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends conversion protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons under the 1981 Act; the only qualified county is currently Hudson County.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership in a qualified county; currently only Hudson County.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","role":"covered","text":"converting buildings in a qualified county (currently only Hudson County)"}],"per_tenancy":"Tenants who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981.","coverage_quotes":["The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted"]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","n_rules":8,"note":null}

